\section{Versions that can change}{

An image tag such as \verb{rocker/r-ver:4.6.1} can be moved to a newer image,
so without \code{digest}, a later build can get newer system libraries. To
pin the base image, read the digest of its multi-platform index:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{docker buildx imagetools inspect rocker/r-ver:4.6.1
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

and pass the \verb{Digest:} value, such as \code{"sha256:bafb..."}, as \code{digest}.
The build also installs Ubuntu packages for the R packages. These come
from Ubuntu's package archive at build time, so they can be newer in a
later build even with \code{digest}.

To keep the built image itself, write it to a file that you can archive:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{docker save -o my-supplement.tar my-supplement
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\verb{docker load -i my-supplement.tar} reads it back. Commit the
\code{Dockerfile} and the \code{.dockerignore}: the Zenodo archive of a GitHub
release then holds them, but not the image.
}

